WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
Jetpack Compose and React Native express identical declarative UI concepts.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.480
They both build interfaces as functions of state.

3
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.520
But beneath the surface, they execute those concepts using distinct internal engines,

4
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.960
one driven by a compiler, the other entirely bound by runtime rules.

5
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.440
Look at these standard counter components.

6
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.960
Compose in Kotlin on the left, React Native in JavaScript on the right.

7
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.280
If we strip away the syntax highlighting, they are architecturally identical.

8
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.040
Both rely on functions to hold memory.

9
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.200
The equivalents here are remember and use state.

10
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.240
But the mechanisms powering those functions differ.

11
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.920
Compose relies on deep compiler level transformations.

12
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.400
Its snapshot system works behind the scenes to track dependencies

13
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.080
and trigger recompositions automatically.

14
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.840
React Native lacks that compiler intervention.

15
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.520
Instead, it relies on strict runtime execution contracts called hooks.

16
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.800
These are plain JavaScript functions executing sequentially.

17
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.240
Moving between these frameworks requires taking a compiler assisted mental model

18
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.200
and adapting it to a rigid runtime environment

19
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.160
without breaking the application's render cycle.

20
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.560
To move from Compose to React,

21
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.200
you must take manual ownership of memory allocation and dependency tracking

22
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.360
that the Kotlin compiler once handled automatically.

23
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.840
Mapping begins with local state.

24
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.200
In Compose, you retain data across render cycles

25
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.800
using a remember block wrapped around mutable state of.

26
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.720
In React, you achieve this directly with the use state hook.

27
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.640
While the purpose is identical, the structural output differs.

28
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.600
Use state returns a tuple containing exactly two elements,

29
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.280
the current value of the state and a dedicated setter function to update it.

30
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.880
Every time a React component re-renders,

31
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.400
the function executes from top to bottom.

32
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.160
When it hits the use state line, it doesn't create a new variable.

33
00:01:55.160 --> 00:02:00.600
It pulls that value from an invisible external array index mapped specifically to that hook.

34
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.800
When you trigger updates, React batches them.

35
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.640
Much like Compose's atomic snapshot system,

36
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.360
group state changes inside event handlers,

37
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:17.040
React 18 automatically batches multiple use state setters into a single render cycle to save performance.

38
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:23.400
However, this runtime execution creates a specific vulnerability known as a stale closure.

39
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.040
Imagine a mechanical arm grabbing an object at the exact millisecond it transforms.

40
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.800
By the time it closes, it has captured obsolete material.

41
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.200
This happens in React when a new state relies on a previous value.

42
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.280
Because React batches updates,

43
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.000
the referenced variable might be scheduled for an update, but not yet re-rendered.

44
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.400
Relying on that variable directly will cause your data to fall out of sync.

45
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.160
To prevent this, React provides the functional updater form.

46
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.520
By passing an arrow function to the setter, like set count C equals C plus one,

47
00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.480
you bypass the local variable and operate directly on the most recent memory address.

48
00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.360
Because React batches these updates at runtime, without compiler oversight,

49
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:13.600
you must proactively use these functional updaters to guarantee interaction with the freshest data.

50
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.000
UI updating state is only half the equation.

51
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.640
You also need a way to store mutable values in the background.

52
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.240
Data that survives re-renders, but does not trigger a render cycle itself when modified.

53
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:32.440
In Compose, you would write, remember, some object and omit the mutable state of wrapper.

54
00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:35.240
In React, you use the use-reth hook.

55
00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.440
The execution contract of use-reth is specific.

56
00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.640
It holds a mutable value on its dot current property across renders,

57
00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.040
but it actively prevents a re-render cycle when that property is modified.

58
00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:50.840
This makes it ideal for background timers or caching data,

59
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.840
but it also serves a secondary purpose, holding references to native view instances

60
00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.840
so you can manipulate them directly.

61
00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.840
This maps to the Compose pattern, where you might combine remember corotine scope

62
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:08.240
with FindViewTree lifecycle owner to interact with underlying Android views.

63
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.840
Use-reth provides an essential escape hatch from React's reactive engine.

64
00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.640
It allows you to manage pure data or interact with native elements

65
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.640
without polluting the render queue.

66
00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.440
That escape hatch brings us to side effects.

67
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.440
These are the bridges between your declarative UI and imperative operations

68
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.440
like fetching network data or querying a local database.

69
00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.240
Compose handles these with launched effect and side effect.

70
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.440
React condenses this logic into a single hook, use-effect,

71
00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.840
but the way these effects are triggered reveals the architectural divergence

72
00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.240
between Compose and React Native.

73
00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.240
This matrix shows how the two systems handle execution logic,

74
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.840
while Compose automatically tracks effect dependencies via its compiler.

75
00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.640
React requires you to manually supply a dependency array

76
00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.640
to dictate exactly when the effect should run.

77
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.040
The first mode involves providing an array containing specific variables.

78
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.040
This mimics launched effect with defined keys.

79
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:13.440
The effect runs once on-mount and then again only when those specific variables change.

80
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.640
The second mode is an empty array.

81
00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:18.040
This maps to launch-defect unit.

82
00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.840
The effect executes strictly once when the component mounts

83
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.240
and optionally runs a cleanup function when it unmounts.

84
00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.040
The third mode is omitting the array entirely.

85
00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.640
This has no direct compose analog.

86
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:34.840
Without an array, the effect runs after every single render sequence.

87
00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.840
Omitting the dependency array unintentionally is a frequent source of bugs in React.

88
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.240
If the effect updates state and the state triggers a render

89
00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:47.240
and the render triggers the effect, you create an infinite render loop.

90
00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:51.240
The dependency array represents the explicit transfer of responsibility

91
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.440
from the compiler to the developer.

92
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.040
It demands precise manual control over the lifecycle of your side effects.

93
00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:02.240
Managing render cycles also means managing the performance cost of rendering.

94
00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:07.040
To mitigate that cost, we use derived state and stabilize our memory references.

95
00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:12.640
Compose uses derived state of to cache computationally expensive values between renders.

96
00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:16.040
React solves this problem with the use memo hook,

97
00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.840
caching the result of an operation until its dependency shift.

98
00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.440
But React introduces a unique callback problem.

99
00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.840
When you pass a standard function down to a child component,

100
00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:32.040
React's runtime generates a new memory address for that function on every parent render.

101
00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:37.040
If that child component is wrapped in a memoization barrier to prevent unnecessary renders,

102
00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:40.240
this new memory address shatters that barrier.

103
00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.840
Because the function's memory addresses new, React assumes the data has changed.

104
00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:50.640
This breaks the child's memo bailout, forcing it to re-render even if logically nothing is different.

105
00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.240
The solution is the use callback hook.

106
00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.640
This acts similarly to how compose preserves lambda instances to avoid thrashing the system.

107
00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.640
Rapping a function and use callback stabilizes its memory address across parent re-renders.

108
00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.440
It tells React to hole held to the original reference,

109
00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.040
successfully protecting the child component's memoization barrier.

110
00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:14.440
In a runtime engine, referential equality dictates performance.

111
00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:20.240
Use memo and use callback are mandatory tools to manually enforce stable references.

112
00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.040
Because React lacks a compiler to enforce where these tools are placed,

113
00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:31.040
the integrity of the architecture relies on developer adherence to a strict contract known as the rules of hooks.

114
00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.040
Rule 1.

115
00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:35.940
Hooks must only be called at the absolute top level of a component.

116
00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.340
They can never be nested inside if statements, for loops, or nested functions,

117
00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:44.640
because React relies on the exact call order to map external memory.

118
00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:45.640
Rule 2.

119
00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:50.140
Hooks can only be called from within React function components or other custom hooks.

120
00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.340
They will fail if invoked from plain JavaScript functions.

121
00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:58.940
To prevent failures, you can use linting tools, specifically the ESLint plugin React hooks,

122
00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:03.340
which will flag these violations at the editor level before the code executes.

123
00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:07.540
Once you respect these rules, advanced compose concepts map predictably.

124
00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:13.340
Complex local state transitions, like using a V-model reducer pattern with sealed classes,

125
00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:18.240
map to React's use reducer hook, and implicit subtree data sharing,

126
00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:23.040
handled in compose by composition local, translates to react context.

127
00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.240
The concepts remain identical.

128
00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:30.040
Transitioning from Jetpack composed to React native requires you to manually enforce

129
00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:35.240
the execution contracts and memory allocations that a compiler previously automated.

